course: dictionary
lesson: 2387
title: Curiously: with interest, or strangely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Curiously is a how-word. It tells you how something is done or how someone feels. It can mean with interest, wanting to find out: «The cat sniffed the box curiously» shows the cat wanted to know what was inside.
explain: It can also mean strangely or oddly. «The house was curiously quiet» means the house was quiet in a way that felt odd, as if something was not right. Read the words around it to see which meaning fits.
explain: Look for a comma at the start of a sentence, as in «Curiously, the gate was open.» Here it means «It is strange that». Swap that in and check it still makes sense, which is a good test for any word in context.
fact: Curiously comes from curious, which means wanting to know or learn about something.
fact: «Curiously» can mean with interest: «She looked at the strange plant curiously.»
fact: «Curiously» can mean strangely: «The room was curiously cold.»
fact: At the start of a sentence, «Curiously, …» means «It is strange that …».
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, Alice looks about her curiously, and says «Curiouser and curiouser!» when things get stranger and stranger.
exercise: «She wonders half sadly, half curiously what will become of her.» What does curiously show here? | Happily, with a big smile | *With interest, wanting to know what will happen | Quickly, without stopping to think | Angrily, with a loud voice
exercise: «The house was curiously quiet.» What does curiously mean here? | *Strangely, in an odd way | Very loudly | Happily, with no problems at all | With great interest in the noise
exercise: Maya says, «Curiously, the gate was locked.» What does curiously mean at the start of this sentence? | The gate was locked by a friend | *It is strange that the gate was locked | The gate was locked very quickly | The gate was locked with great interest
test: «The puppy sniffed the new toy curiously.» What does curiously tell you about the puppy? | The puppy did not like walking | The puppy was angry with the toy | *The puppy wanted to find out about the toy | The puppy was fast asleep
test: «Curiously, nobody came to the party.» Which sentence means the same? | *It is strange that nobody came to the party | The party was loud and full of fun | Nobody came because it was cold outside | Everybody came early to the party
exam: In «The cat sniffed the box curiously», which sentence best explains curiously? | The cat was angry and hissed at the box | The cat was asleep in the box | The cat jumped over the box | *The cat wanted to find out what was in the box
